<header><p class="st-kicker">city state · Anatolia and the Levant</p><h1 class="st-title">Sidon</h1><p class="st-meta">1500 BCE – 332 BCE (approx.) · capital Sidon</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Sidon, one of the oldest Phoenician cities, whose kings led the Persian navy and whose name long stood for all Phoenicians.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Sidon stood on a promontory with offshore islets forming a harbour on the southern Lebanese coast. Inhabited since prehistory, it was a Canaanite port in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akhetaten">Amarna</a> age, and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/homer">Homer</a> and the Bible Sidonians means <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phoenicians">Phoenicians</a> in general, a sign of its early prestige.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Its kings ruled a merchant city famous for glass and purple cloth, and honoured the healing god <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eshmun">Eshmun</a> at a great sanctuary outside the city. Under the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/achaemenid-empire">Achaemenid Empire</a> Sidon was the leading Phoenician city and supplied the core of the Persian fleet; the inscribed sarcophagus of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eshmunazar-ii">Eshmunazar II</a> survives.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/esarhaddon">Esarhaddon</a> of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/assyrian-empire">Assyrian Empire</a> razed the city in 677 BCE, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/artaxerxes-iii">Artaxerxes III</a> burned it again after King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tennes">Tennes</a> rebelled. In 332 BCE Sidon opened its gates to Alexander of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/macedonian-empire">Macedonian Empire</a>, who installed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abdalonymus">Abdalonymus</a> as king, ending its independent history.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/sidon.jpg" alt="Sidon" width="320" height="240"><figcaption>Image: Vyacheslav Argenberg · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:View_from_Sidon%27s_Sea_Castle,_Sidon,_Lebanon.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1500 BCE – 332 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/16th-century-bce">16th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/4th-century-bce">4th century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>city state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Sidon</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Eshmunazar II, Tennes, Abdalonymus</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Phoenician religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Phoenician</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/lebanon">Lebanon</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Kingdom of Sidon, Saida</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-916&amp;focus=sidon" aria-label="Sidon on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5583 960 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Sidon lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5903 1216L5863 1489M5903 1216L6092 1134M5903 1216L6140 1251M5903 1216L5548 987"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5863 1489h0M6092 1134h0M6140 1251h0M5548 987h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5903 1216h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5903 1216h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=-916&amp;focus=sidon">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

